concept :the_fouling_round do
  name "The Fouling Round"
  aka "the quarter"
  tags :ecology, :military, :catastrophe, :water
  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    Every quarter, every surface inside a #{ref :the_mediterranean_chain, "Chain"} enclave's wall that meets water or wet air is graded for what is growing on it. Drone skins, hulls, gate rams and their seats, reef anchors, culvert throats, pump interiors, ladder rungs below the tide mark, and the barrier's own beds. A surveyor walks the list with a #{ref :comparison_tiles, "tile set"} and writes one grade against each surface with the fraction of its area that carries it.

    The grade runs from nought to a hundred in ten steps. Up to thirty is film and soft growth that comes off under a brush. From forty upward the growth is anchored, and taking it off takes substrate with it.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :mechanics, heading: "Grade And Area"
    A surface is pulled from service on the fourth step across a tenth of its area, on the third step across a fifth, or on the third and fourth together across a fifth. A whole hull at the second step is left in the water; a gate seat at the fourth across a hand's breadth of its bearing face is pulled the same day.

    Grading is by comparison and not by instrument. The surveyor lays the tile against the surface under a raking lamp, then drags a leather pad across both, and the grade is the tile the surface answers like. Nothing in the procedure reports anywhere and nothing in it is alive, which is why the round survived the arrival of machinery that could have done it faster.

    A pulled article goes to a #{ref :kalkara_bake_shed, "bake shed"}, comes apart into pieces that fit an oven, and comes back or does not.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "What May Go In The Water"
    A new surface cannot be put in the water until it has been lapped below the roughness the finest tile in the set carries. Anchored growth needs something to hold, and what a nail can feel is enough to hold it. Cast faces, weld beads, worn keyways and any seam a pad catches on are lapped, filled or refused.

    The round therefore decides what the enclaves buy. Machinery that comes apart into bakeable pieces passes the shed; anything with an oiled bearing, a seated gasket or a closed void that cannot be opened is refused at the quay, whatever it does. This is why #{ref :sterile_machines, "sterile machinery"} out of the Chinese resistance shops commands its price on the Chain, and it is bought there by enclaves that would not otherwise deal with resistance suppliers at all.

    The barrier is graded like everything else. A #{ref :severed_strains, "severed strain"} that has crept out of its bed onto the wall behind it is entered at a grade and cut back to the bed, and the gate crews grade the culvert throats every quarter because a #{ref :the_others, "landing"} that establishes inside a wall establishes on anchored growth. The #{future "Marsaskala fleet"} holds that the round is a tax on hulls and that growth on a hull costs a knot and nothing else, and pays it under protest at every quarter.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — The scale, the comparison method and the threshold come from naval hull fouling ratings, a discipline the enclaves' surveyors do not otherwise practise: a nought-to-a-hundred rating in ten defined steps, soft growth in the low bands and hard growth in the high, judged by comparing the surface against reference standards, with cleaning triggered by a grade-and-area pair — the fourth step over a tenth of the hull, the third over a fifth, or the two together over a fifth. Borrowed because it makes the decision to pull an article an argument about area rather than about severity, so a small patch on a bearing face outranks a whole hull under film."
  log "2026-08-09 — The certification limit for a new surface is a roughness figure, from hygienic equipment design: product-contact surfaces are specified at or below a stated roughness because below it bacterial adhesion drops and cleaning becomes reliable. Borrowed because it puts the whole argument about what may enter the water into something a surveyor can settle with a pad and a tile instead of a judgement about the organism."
end
